Subject: windows.ui.xaml: Viewbox, VisualStateManager.GoToState, bindings to an app class's own properties

Viewbox measures its Child at its natural size and scales it to fit by Stretch and StretchDirection. The child keeps
its natural layout and is scaled by a layout scale on the element, which drawing and hit testing apply after its
RenderTransform, as WinUI's Viewbox does with a scale transform.

Markup and {Binding}s now reach the properties an app class composing an element declares itself (a UserControl's
Device or Type): the element remembers the app class markup created it as, and the value goes through the app's
IXamlMember (its metadata provider) for that class, or to a DependencyProperty the app registered under that name.
.NET Native's objects don't report their class names, so the recorded one is used.

VisualStateManager's statics are registered. GoToState switches the states of a control's template or, for a
UserControl, of its content root, whose named elements are the setters' targets; states apply without transitions.
GetVisualStateGroups and custom visual state managers are not supported.

